    "updated_at": "2025-05-28T07:27:08Z",
    "body": "Would prefer it to be independent of further dependencies. \nQuimb is, however, an interesting option to integrate as a backend, usage would be like this\n\n```python\ncompiled_circuit = tq.compile(circuit, backend=\"quimb\")\nmatrix = compiled_circuit.to_matrix()\n```\n\nthis would require a `simulator_quimb.py` interface in `src/tequila/simulators`.\n\nI would assume that is more work then the numpy way at the top level, but would also be more valuable in the end. \n\nFurther alternative: implement as above using quimb but with imports such that it will not require to have quimb installed. \n(basically the same as the `to_networkx` function in [`src/tequila/circuit/circuit.py`](https://github.com/tequilahub/tequila/blob/master/src/tequila/circuit/circuit.py))\n\n",
    "author_association": "COLLABORATOR",
    "pin": null,
